  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5+y/2}-9 & =21 \\\\ {e}^{5+y/2} & =21+9 \\\\ {e}^{5+y/2} & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5+y/2} & =30 \\\\ ln\u2061({e}^{5+y/2}) & =ln\u2061(30) \\\\ 5+\\frac{y}{2} & =ln\u2061(30) \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =ln\u2061(30)-5 \\\\ y & =2ln\u2061(30)-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny\u2248-3.198\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
